{"headline":"Strength is climbing across every muscle group — you had a fantastic week.","summary":"This was a genuinely strong period. Every single muscle group saw its strength index rise, tonnage was solid at over 9,000 kg across 24 sets, and your lifts are moving in exactly the right direction for your goal of staying strong and healthy. For a 46-year-old woman training consistently, these numbers reflect both good effort and smart recovery. Keep building on this momentum.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"on_track","notes":"Every muscle group improved its strength index this period. Legs showed the biggest jump, rising from 105 to 110, closely followed by glutes (104 to 108) and back (103 to 107). Chest also made a meaningful gain from 102 to 106. Shoulders and core moved more modestly, which is normal given their supporting role and lower volume. Across the board, progressive overload is clearly happening — this is exactly the trend you want to sustain."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"shoulders","severity":"mild","observation":"Shoulders account for only 9.1% of weekly volume and recorded the smallest strength index gain this period. While this is not alarming, it is worth monitoring to ensure shoulder health and pressing stability are maintained long-term."},{"muscleGroup":"core","severity":"mild","observation":"Core sits at just 2% of total volume, the lowest of any group. Core strength supports nearly all compound lifts and injury resilience, so a slight increase in dedicated core work could pay dividends over time."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Keep your current loading on legs and glutes since they are progressing fastest — avoid the temptation to jump weight too quickly and prioritize clean form to protect your joints.","Add one or two extra sets of shoulder work next period, such as a lateral raise or overhead press variation, to bring shoulder volume more in line with the rest of your training.","Include at least two dedicated core exercises per session, such as planks or dead bugs, to nudge core volume above the current 2% share and support your overall strength base.","Continue monitoring recovery between sessions — at 46, quality sleep and protein intake are key levers for sustaining this kind of across-the-board progress week after week."],"contextSummary":"Exceptional week with strength index gains across all six muscle groups and solid tonnage; shoulders and core remain mild imbalances to address by adding volume next period."}